    "case_id": "736",
    "case_summary": "A 65-year-old male with a history of myasthenia gravis, hypertension, chronic kidney disease, and diabetes presented with 40 pounds of weight loss over three months, cough without hemoptysis, and low back pain. He denied fevers or chills. Patient had stopped taking his prescribed mycophenolate and pyridostigmine medications for myasthenia gravis.\n\nPhysical exam was notable for mild left flank tenderness.\n\n- Laboratory tests:    - BUN >200 mg/dL    - Creatinine >11 mg/dL (previously <1 mg/dL in 2017)    - Elevated lipase with normal liver function tests    - Hemoglobin 7.8 g/dL    - Positive COVID PCR    - ESR 119 mm/h    - CRP 93 mg/L    - False-positive cocci IgG    - Positive quantiferon gold    - Positive urine culture for group B streptococcus  - Imaging studies:    - Chest X-ray: Pulmonary consolidations at medial and posterior-bilateral infrahilar zones    - CT abdomen/pelvis: Severe bilateral hydroureteronephrosis with enlarged prostate, nonspecific bilateral perinephric stranding, and possible destructive changes at L4 and L5    - Maxillofacial CT: Showed periodontal disease    - MRI lumbar spine:      Image Title: Lumbar Spine MRI      Image Description: Shows discitis at L4-L5 level without epidural phlegmon or abscess, edema in bilateral posterior paravertebral muscles, myositis involving bilateral psoas muscles without abscess      - Pathology/Other:    - CT-guided spinal biopsy: Acute and chronic inflammation with micro abscess formation, necrosis, and rare hemosiderin-laden histiocytes    - UW universal PCR test: Positive for S. cristatus    - Cultures: No growth",
